Radiculopathy – Cervical
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Dermatomal radiating arm pain, Myotomal weakness, Positive Spurling's test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-050
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing, electric-shock pain shooting from the neck down the arm, causing sudden weakness and inability to sleep or hold objects.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing severe, unremitting, sharp radiating pain down the upper limb accompanied by acute motor weakness and numbness. Urgent assessment was required to definitively evaluate for acute, severe nerve root compression versus a central neurological event, and to administer potent, targeted neuropathic analgesia and systemic anti-inflammatories to arrest the acute nerve ischemia prior to daytime MRI services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is an agonizing electric shock shooting from my neck all the way into my thumb; my arm feels dead, and I keep dropping things."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe onset of right-sided cervical radicular pain. Timeline: Woke up 24 hours ago with a stiff neck; rapidly progressed over the last 6 hours into a unilateral, sharp, shooting, electric shock-like pain radiating strictly from the right neck down into the shoulder, lateral arm, and into the thumb and index finger (C6 distribution). Exacerbation of radiating symptoms is severely triggered by any active neck extension or rightward rotation. Patient reports profound numbness and tingling mapped exactly to the corresponding sensory dermatome. Notes acute, objective motor weakness, complaining of an inability to firmly grasp objects. Patient found significant relief of radiating arm pain earlier by resting the hand on top of the head (positive shoulder abduction sign), but the pain has now broken through.
Functional Status: Highly impaired right upper limb function; unable to sleep, dress, or grip safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation and sudden functional loss of the dominant arm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 146/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, in severe distress, holding the right hand rigidly on top of the head (abduction sign) to seek relief.
Physical Exam: Detailed neurological examination of the upper limbs reveals distinct right-sided pathology. Sensory exam demonstrates decreased light touch and pinprick strictly in the C6 dermatome (lateral forearm, thumb, and index finger). Motor testing reveals 4/5 weakness specifically in right wrist extension and elbow flexion (C6 myotome). Reflex testing demonstrates a diminished (1+) right brachioradialis reflex compared to the left (2+). Distinctly positive Spurling's test: careful axial compression of the slightly extended and rightward-bent cervical spine forcefully and immediately reproduces the electric shooting pain down into the thumb. Active neck range of motion is heavily guarded. Lower limb neurology is completely normal.
Environmental Observations: N/A
Medication Reconciliation: Took regular Paracetamol and Ibuprofen with zero effect on the shooting nerve pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Right Cervical Radiculopathy (C6 Nerve Root Compression) - Status: Acute/Severe
Differentials:

Stroke / Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) - Ruled out definitively by the strictly peripheral, single-nerve root dermatomal/myotomal distribution, classical mechanical provocation (Spurling's), relief with positioning, and absence of cranial nerve or lower limb deficits.

Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - Ruled out by the presence of severe neck pain, positive Spurling's test, diminished brachioradialis reflex (which originates proximal to the carpal tunnel), and motor weakness extending above the wrist (elbow flexion).

Parsonage-Turner Syndrome (Brachial Neuritis) - Less likely given the classic mechanical provocation signs and immediate response to physical positioning (abduction sign), which point to structural compression rather than primary viral neuritis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, severe acute cervical radiculopathy (C6), characterized by excruciating electric-shock pain, distinct dermatomal numbness, and specific myotomal weakness. The critical clinical task was to map the neurological deficits to confirm a peripheral nerve root compression and exclude a central demyelinating or ischemic event. Clinical examination firmly established the focal, mechanical nature of the C6 compression (positive Spurling's, diminished specific reflex). The severity of the neuropathic pain rendered standard analgesics useless and completely disrupted sleep. Urgent after-hours assessment safely provided diagnostic validation, bypassing an unnecessary stroke workup in the ED, and allowed for the immediate initiation of high-dose systemic steroids and neuropathic agents to rapidly decompress the inflamed nerve root.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Prednisolone 25mg STAT (as a rapid systemic anti-inflammatory to reduce nerve root edema). Prescribed oral Pregabalin 75mg BD to modulate the severe neuropathic shooting pain. Advised to continue Ibuprofen 400mg TDS.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Sleep and rest strictly with the affected hand placed on top of your head or resting high on a stack of pillows next to you. Consequence: Immediate physical slacking of the stretched nerve root, drastically reducing the crushing pressure in the neck and stopping the agonizing electrical shocks (negative reinforcement).
Action: Wear a soft cervical collar strictly for the next 48 hours, but only when sitting up or walking. Consequence: The collar acts as a physical reminder to prevent sudden neck extension or turning, avoiding the mechanical pinching that triggers the severe pain spikes (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a soft cervical collar for temporary postural feedback.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48-72 hours to arrange a Cervical Spine MRI to identify the structural cause (disc herniation vs. osteophyte) and assess the progressing motor weakness.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden, total loss of movement in the arm, if the weakness spreads to the legs, or if there is any new loss of bowel or bladder control.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis securely identifies nerve root compression based on clinical neurology. An MRI is absolutely required to visualize the exact structural cause (slipped disc vs. bone spur). Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The electric pain should reduce significantly over 3-5 days as the steroids shrink the swelling around the trapped nerve. However, the numbness and weakness may take weeks or months to fully resolve depending on the severity of the compression.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if your arm becomes completely paralyzed, if your legs become heavy or weak, or if you lose control of your bladder.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A disc or bone spur in your neck is aggressively pinching the main nerve cable that supplies your arm (the C6 nerve). This is causing the electrical misfiring, numbness, and weakness. Standard painkillers do not work for nerve compression. The high-dose steroid will rapidly shrink the swelling around the nerve, while the new nerve medication calms down the electrical shocks.
Actionable Steps: Keep your hand resting on top of your head whenever possible; this physically pulls the nerve away from the pinch point. Do not look up at the ceiling or turn your head sharply to the right. You must get an MRI organized by your GP this week.

